[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Stored Procedures, Functions, and Dynamic SQL in Microsoft SQL Server"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering programmability in Microsoft SQL Server is essential for building scalable, secure, and high-performing database applications. This tutorial explores Stored Procedures, User-Defined Functions (UDFs), and Dynamic SQL, detailing their architectures, best practices, and security implications."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Stored Procedures"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Stored procedures are precompiled collections of Transact-SQL (T-SQL) statements stored in the database. They promote code reuse, improve execution plan caching, and mitigate SQL injection risks when parameters are used correctly."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating and Executing a Stored Procedure"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"CREATE PROCEDURE sp_GetEmployeeDetails
    @DepartmentID INT,
    @MinSalary DECIMAL(18, 2)
AS
BEGIN
    SET NOCOUNT ON;

    SELECT EmployeeID, FirstName, LastName, Salary
    FROM Employees
    WHERE DepartmentID = @DepartmentID
      AND Salary >= @MinSalary;
END;
GO

-- Execution
EXEC sp_GetEmployeeDetails @DepartmentID = 3, @MinSalary = 50000.00;"},{"order":7,"type":"heading","text":"2. User-Defined Functions (UDFs)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Functions in SQL Server encapsulate repeatable logic and return either a single scalar value or a table. Unlike stored procedures, functions can be utilized directly inside SQL queries within SELECT, WHERE, and JOIN clauses."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Scalar and Table-Valued Functions"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"-- Scalar Function
CREATE FUNCTION dbo.fn_CalculateAnnualCompensation
(
    @MonthlySalary DECIMAL(18,2),
    @BonusPercentage DECIMAL(5,2)
)
RETURNS DECIMAL(18,2)
AS
BEGIN
    RETURN (@MonthlySalary * 12) * (1 + (@BonusPercentage / 100.0));
END;
GO

-- Inline Table-Valued Function (ITVF)
CREATE FUNCTION dbo.fn_GetActiveEmployeesByDept
(
    @DeptID INT
)
RETURNS TABLE
AS
RETURN
(
    SELECT EmployeeID, FirstName, LastName, HireDate
    FROM Employees
    WHERE DepartmentID = @DeptID AND IsActive = 1
);
GO"},{"order":11,"type":"heading","text":"3. Dynamic SQL"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dynamic SQL allows you to construct and execute T-SQL statements dynamically at runtime. This is useful when writing generic stored procedures that require flexible column names, sorting directives, or dynamic table targets. The system stored procedure sp_executesql should always be preferred over the legacy EXEC('string') method because it supports parameterization and execution plan reuse."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Executing Dynamic SQL Securely with sp_executesql"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"CREATE PROCEDURE sp_SearchEmployeesDynamic
    @SearchColumn VARCHAR(50),
    @SearchValue VARCHAR(100)
AS
BEGIN
    SET NOCOUNT ON;

    DECLARE @SQL NVARCHAR(MAX);
    DECLARE @ParamDefinition NVARCHAR(500);

    -- Validate column name against metadata to prevent SQL injection
    IF @SearchColumn NOT IN ('FirstName', 'LastName', 'Email')
    BEGIN
        RAISERROR('Invalid search column specified.', 16, 1);
        RETURN;
    END

    -- Construct the dynamic query safely
    SET @SQL = N'SELECT EmployeeID, FirstName, LastName, Email '
             + N'FROM Employees '
             + N'WHERE ' + QUOTENAME(@SearchColumn) + N' LIKE @Value;';

    SET @ParamDefinition = N'@Value VARCHAR(100)';

    -- Execute parameterized dynamic SQL
    EXEC sp_executesql @SQL, @ParamDefinition, @Value = @SearchValue;
END;
GO"},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices and Performance Considerations"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always include SET NOCOUNT ON at the beginning of stored procedures to reduce unnecessary network traffic caused by rowcount messages."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Favor Inline Table-Valued Functions (ITVFs) over Multi-Statement Table-Valued Functions (MSTVFs) as ITVFs allow the query optimizer to unroll and integrate the execution plan."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never concatenate user input directly into dynamic SQL strings. Always use sp_executesql and utilize QUOTENAME() for database identifiers."}]
